WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
Good Manufacturing Practice requires
manufacturing operations to follow approved

2
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.520
instructions and appropriate controls.
Processes should be defined, suitable for

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.800
their intended purpose, and
performed by trained personnel.

4
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.640
Controls must address potential contamination,
mix-ups and errors throughout the

5
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:22.160
process. This introductory lesson
explains common operating principles.

6
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.981
Specific requirements depend on the
product, dosage form and manufacturing

7
00:00:26.981 --> 00:00:31.740
process. Incoming starting materials
and packaging components should be

8
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.240
received, identified, and controlled
according to approved procedures.

9
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:41.020
Appropriate quarantine and release
arrangements help prevent unintended use.

10
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.760
Storage conditions and stock rotation
should reflect material requirements.

11
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.300
Before dispensing, personnel should
verify material identity, status and

12
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.480
suitability. A label alone may not
be sufficient when supporting records

13
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.580
indicate a discrepancy. Dispensing involves
selecting and measuring the correct

14
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:05.000
approved materials for a defined batch.
Operators should follow authorized

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.720
instructions, use suitable calibrated
instruments, and document quantities and

16
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.000
material identities. Applicable
independent checks must be performed.

17
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:20.240
European Union GMP requires an independent
check of each dispensed material and

18
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.820
its weight or volume,
with the check recorded.

19
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:28.780
United States regulations specify
second-person checks, with defined exceptions

20
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:34.000
for qualifying automated equipment. A
local risk assessment does not waive

21
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.040
applicable requirements. Traceability
connects each dispensed material to its

22
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.440
source and the batch in which it is
used. Before manufacturing begins, line

23
00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:48.020
clearance and setup checks help ensure
that previous materials, labels,

24
00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.020
documents and residues do not create
mix-up or contamination risks.

25
00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:57.680
The equipment, area and materials should
be confirmed suitable for the planned

26
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.840
operation. Checks must follow approved
procedures and be recorded as required.

27
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.200
The exact sequence and verification
method depend on the facility and process.

28
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.320
In-process controls provide information
about the state of a manufacturing

29
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:18.380
process while it is running. Examples
may include weight, volume, temperature,

30
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.740
pressure, pH, appearance or other
process-specific measurements.

31
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:27.500
Monitoring frequency, limits and response
actions must come from the approved

32
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.580
process and supporting
scientific justification.

33
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:35.720
There is no single universal acceptance
criterion that applies to every

34
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:40.640
pharmaceutical process. Batch manufacturing
records provide traceable evidence

35
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.540
of the actual operations performed.
Entries should identify relevant materials,

36
00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:51.840
equipment, operators, times, process
observations and checks as required by the

37
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.520
approved record. Reconciliation can
help detect unexplained differences in

38
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.260
quantities or printed materials.
Documentation must reflect what occurred,

39
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.280
including deviations, and must not be
completed retrospectively from guesswork.

40
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.640
Unexpected process results, equipment
alarms or procedural departures should be

41
00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.020
handled under the site quality system.
Operators should secure the process as

42
00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.580
appropriate, report the event
and preserve relevant data.

43
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:24.600
Authorized personnel assess product
impact and determine investigation,

44
00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.180
disposition and corrective actions. A
result that later returns to normal does

45
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.200
not automatically eliminate the need
to document and evaluate the original

46
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.780
event. Imagine an operator preparing
to start a batch who discovers that a

47
00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.420
container of starting material has a
released label but the electronic inventory

48
00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.760
record still shows quarantine status.
The operator should not choose whichever

49
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.640
status seems convenient. The material
should be held from use while authorized

50
00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.600
personnel reconcile the discrepancy
under approved procedures.

51
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:04.760
This illustrates why status verification,
traceability and escalation matter

52
00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.520
before manufacturing begins.
